Securepath Log
==============

____
Fast and user friendly logging component to log anything, anytime and anywhere.
____

<link_to_github_repo_path>

== Info

Default logging setup uses std::fstream.

== Performance


== Content
 
default_logger.hpp::
  Default logger that formats message and uses 'basic_backend' to write message to log.
log.hpp::
  Contains macros 'LOG_TRACE', 'LOG_INFO' and 'LOG_WARN' that can be used to log messages.
log_handler.hpp::
  Contains functions called by macros defined in log.hpp.
types.hpp::
  Types used in log library.
util.hpp::
  Helper functions.
backend/backend.hpp::
  Backend interface.
backend/backend.cpp::
  Backend implementation.
backend/config.hpp::
  Backend settings.
backend/file_output.hpp::
  Derived class of 'output' that logs messages to file.
backend/output.hpp::
  Base class for outputs that can be added to 'basic_backend'.

== Basic usage

Include following headers in file where you want to create backend for logging component (for example in main.cpp):

[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/log/backend/backend.hpp>
#include <securepath/log/backend/file_output.hpp>
....

Create 'basic_backend' and 'file_output' that logs messages to file whose name given as parameter. Name is used to distinguish outputs from each others when using multiple outputs.
[source, c++]
....
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("name", "logfile.log");
....

Include header containing macros for logging in source file where you want to log something:
[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/log/log.hpp>
....

Logging can be done by using macros defined in 'log.hpp', for example:

[source, c++]
....
LOG_TRACE("simple message");
LOG_INFO("{} == {} is {} {} ({})", 1, 2, "obviously", "false", false);

try {
  // ...
} catch(std::exception const& e) {
  LOG_WARN("Error: %", e.what());
}
....

Backend can be removed by using remove function
[source, c++]
....
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<log::backend::file_output>(name, file.log);
LOG_TRACE("test");
securepath::log::backend::remove(name);
LOG_TRACE("test2");
// file.log content is now "test"
....

== Example implementation (basic)

main.cpp
[source, c++]
....
#include "functions.hpp"
#include <securepath/log/backend/backend.hpp>
#include <securepath/log/backend/file_output.hpp>
#include <securepath/log/log.hpp> // needed here because of LOG_WARN in catch-block

int main(int argc, char* args[]) {
	int ret = 0;
	try {
		securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("file", "logfile.log");
    LOG_INFO("info");
		log_string("write this to log");
		calculate(0);
		calculate(1);
	} catch(std::exception const& e) {
		LOG_WARN("Error: %", e.what());
	}
	return ret;
}
....

funtions.hpp
[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/log/log.hpp>

inline
void log_string(std::string s) {
	LOG_INFO("logged: %", s);
}

inline
void calculate(int x) {
	if(x == 0) {
		LOG_WARN("Calculate function - trying to divide by zero");
	}
}
....

logfile.log content is now:
....
<data and time> main.cpp:10         4 info
<date and time> functions.hpp:5     0 logged: write this to log
<date and time> functions.hpp:11    7 Calculate function - trying to divide by zero
....

== Advanced usage

=== Multiple outputs
Using multiple outputs, use them like mention in 'basic usage'
[source, c++]
....
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("name1", "file1.log");
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("name2", "file2.log");
....

=== Log levels
Class 'output' has function 'get_min_log_level()' that tells the minimum log level euch output is logging. For example if having added outputs
[source, c++]
....
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("name1", "file1.log"); // default: 0
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("name2", "file2.log", 1); // 1
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<securepath::log::backend::file_output>("name3", "file3.log", 5); // 5
....
Now for example LOG_INFO would not go to 'file3.log' because name3's minumum log level (5) is higher than LOG_INFO's level (4).

=== Custom backend

Implement derived class of 'output.hpp'
[source, c++]
....
struct custom_output : public securepath::log::backend::output_base {

  custom_output();
  virtual void log(formatted_message const& m);
  virtual int get_min_log_level() const;

};
....

And add it to basic backend
[source, c++]
....
// whether
securepath::log::backend::add_backend<custom_output>("name", "file.log");
// or
securepath::log::backend::add("name", std::make_unique<custom_output>("file.log"));
....
=== Custom logger

'log_handler.hpp' defines second function that can be used instead of default one. This function can be used by giving reference to custom logger as first log macro parameter:

[source, c++]
....
void write_to_log(Logger& logger) {
  LOG_INFO(logger, "using custom logger to log this message");
}
....

Custom logger must have functions 'get_min_log_level()' and 'log()', as well as define type 'logger_type'. For example:
[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/util/print_util.hpp>
// #include ...

struct custom_logger {
  
  using logger_type = std::ofstream; // Don't forget 'using logger_type'!
  
  custom_logger(std::string const& file)
  : out_(file, std::ios_base::ate)
  {}

  template<typename... Args>
  void log(log_info const& info, char const* format, Args const&... args) {
    std::string s = std::string(info.file) + " " + std::to_string(info.line) + print(format, args...);
  	out_ << s << std::endl;
  }

  int get_min_log_level() {
	return 0; // 'log everything'
  }
private:
	std::ofstream out_;
};
....

=== log_info

log_info ('types.hpp') contains information about where (file and line) log macro was called as well as type of the message as level. These levels are mentioned in 'log.hpp' - 0 for 'LOG_TRACE', 4 for 'LOG_INFO' and 7 for 'LOG_WARN'.
